            </Main>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

This older 1984 house has a lot of excessive air leakage, as outlined below:



** All of the original wood windows have rotted and will no longer close tightly



** The rear door does not close well &amp; the front door is not well aligned to seal when closed



** The 6" chimney &amp; 5" combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room will both be eliminated when you upgrade the boiler



** Some leakage through the bathroom exhaust fan



Savings: In conjunction with your planned upgrades for this house (new windows, doors &amp; a direct-vent combi boiler), you should achieve at significant reduction in natural air leakage. I estimate a 40% reduction is realistic, that could potentially save $ 645 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 26.0 GJ.</airSeal>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house are original to the 1984 construction of your house, and the wood frames have deteriorated to the point where they no longer close well ........ resulting in significant air leakage heat loss. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of the windows (including the front patio door &amp; the 2 skylights) with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows (except for the skylights), with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour. You mentioned the front patio door may be replaced with a window unit &amp; this is what I modeled for the upgrade.



Savings:

By replacing all of the above windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 187 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.5 GJ. Please note - these savings only reflect the better energy performance of the new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. For your house, this upgrade will also significantly reduce air leakage heat loss from your house ............ and those savings are outlined later in this report.



There are currently no rebates for upgrading windows, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), which requires an eligible wall insulation &amp; siding upgrade to the house. We talked about that, and this is not something you are currently planning to do. Based on the R20 wall insulation your house currently has, I don't think an exterior wall insulation upgrade would be cost-effective for you UNLESS you were already planning to replace the siding.

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that also heats your domestic hot water, with a 65 litre external storage  buffer tank. The boiler appears to be original to when the house was built.



As discussed, you are planning to upgrade to a new ENERGY STAR?????? propane combi boiler (you had a quote for an NTI VMax 153P). I have modeled the estimated energy &amp; cost savings associated of this 95% AFUE combi boiler to both heat your house and provide high efficiency (0.87 EF) on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 6" chimney or the 5" combustion air supply pipe (removing both of these pipes will significantly reduce the air leakage heat loss from your house ............ and those savings are outlined later in this report.



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 958 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 41.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the one bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of only 0.1 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent - your current boiler is chimney vented.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see a carbon monoxide (CO) detector? Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - 1 bathroom fan

        - A clothes dryer



Overall, your house currently has excess air leakage (heat loss) and the renovations you have planned (and the information outlined in this report) will help you to reduce this air leakage - saving you energy &amp; money, while still maintaining an adequate indoor air quality.



If you decide to replace the bathroom fan, please select an Energy Star rated unit. You may want to consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
